<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  AWS CardDemo - Logback configuration (Spring Boot 3.2.x / Logback 1.4.x)
  ============================================================================

  Responsibilities
  ================
  1. Console + rolling-file appenders for the migrated CardDemo application.
  2. Per-profile log levels via Spring Boot's <springProfile> extension
     (dev / prod / and a fallback for any other profile such as "test").
  3. Defense-in-depth PII suppression: the card CVV and the full customer SSN
     MUST NEVER appear in logs, even if an upstream layer accidentally logs an
     entity/DTO that still carries them.

  Why this file is named "logback-spring.xml" (NOT "logback.xml")
  ===============================================================
  Spring Boot only enables the <springProfile> and <springProperty> extension
  elements when the configuration file is named "logback-spring.xml" and is
  loaded by Spring Boot's LogbackLoggingSystem (SpringBootJoranConfigurator).
  Renaming this file to "logback.xml" would silently disable profile handling.

  PII masking - source of truth & rules
  =====================================
  * CARD-CVV-CD  PIC 9(03)  (app/cpy/CVACT02Y.cpy) - 3-digit card verification
    value. AAP 0.6.8 / 0.7.1: MUST NOT be serialized or logged.
  * CUST-SSN     PIC 9(09)  (app/cpy/CVCUS01Y.cpy) - 9-digit SSN. May be exposed
    at most as its last 4 digits; the full SSN MUST NOT appear in logs.
  Masking here is the *secondary* control (a safety net). The *primary* control
  is that the DTO/mapper layer never emits these fields (AAP 0.3.2, 0.6.8).

  NOTE: the customer id CUST-ID is also PIC 9(09) (9 digits). A blanket
  "mask every 9 consecutive digits" rule would therefore destroy legitimate
  customer ids in the logs. We deliberately mask only (a) values explicitly
  labelled "cvv"/"ssn" and (b) SSNs written in the dashed ddd-dd-dddd form,
  which preserves customer/account ids while still suppressing the sensitive
  fields in the realistic accidental-logging cases (field labels, toString()).

  ============================================================================
  CRITICAL ESCAPING NOTE - DO NOT "FIX" THE BACKSLASHES TO DOUBLE BACKSLASHES
  ============================================================================
  Logback's %replace(...) conversion word passes the regex option through to
  java.util.regex VERBATIM - it performs NO backslash unescaping. Therefore:
    * regex metacharacters use a SINGLE backslash: \d  \s  \b
    * a LITERAL backslash in the regex uses a DOUBLE backslash: \\
  Writing \\d here (intending the digit class) would reach the regex engine as
  "literal-backslash followed by d", which never matches real text and SILENTLY
  DISABLES MASKING. This escaping was verified at runtime against Logback 1.4.14
  by emitting cvv=123 / "cvv":"1234" / ssn=123-45-6789 / ssn=123456789 and
  confirming the output is masked. XML entities: &quot; = " , &amp; = & .
  ============================================================================
-->
<configuration>

    <!--
      Spring Boot default conventions: registers the clr/wex/wEx/applicationName
      conversion rules and the standard CONSOLE_LOG_PATTERN / charset properties,
      plus noise-reducing logger levels. Safe to include; our custom pattern
      below does not depend on the colour converters.
    -->
    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

    <!-- Log directory + file. Override the directory via the LOG_PATH env var. -->
    <property name="LOG_PATH" value="${LOG_PATH:-./logs}"/>
    <property name="LOG_FILE" value="${LOG_PATH}/carddemo.log"/>

    <!--
      PII / credential masking pattern (defense-in-depth). Seven nested %replace
      calls are applied to %msg, innermost first:
        1) CVV           : a "cvv"-labelled 3-4 digit value               -> ***
        2) SSN           : a "ssn"-labelled 9-digit value (dashes opt.)   -> ***-**-****
        3) bare          : a dashed SSN of the form ddd-dd-dddd           -> ***-**-****
        4) password      : a "password"-labelled value                   -> ****
        5) token         : a "token"-labelled value (incl. access_token) -> ****
        6) bearer        : a "Bearer &lt;credential&gt;" value           -> ****
        7) authorization : "Authorization: Bearer|Basic &lt;cred&gt;"    -> ****
      The CVV/SSN separator class [\\&quot;\s:=] matches a backslash, a double quote,
      whitespace, ':' or '=', so labels written as cvv=123, "cvv":"123",
      \"cvv\":\"123\", cvv: 123 etc. are all covered. (?i) = case-insensitive.

      The credential masks (4-7) require at least one separator AFTER the label (the
      '+' quantifier) so ordinary words such as "passwordless" are NOT masked, and
      they bound the secret with [^\s&quot;,&amp;]+ (stops at whitespace, a double
      quote, a comma or an ampersand) so only the value is replaced - this masks the
      ENTIRE secret regardless of its inner characters (e.g. p@ss!w0rd#) while leaving
      adjacent fields, JSON quotes (&quot;password&quot;:&quot;****&quot;) and URL
      params (password=****&amp;next=...) intact. Mask (6) handles both a bare
      "Bearer &lt;jwt&gt;" and the credential inside an "Authorization: Bearer
      &lt;jwt&gt;" header; mask (7) is scheme-anchored (Bearer|Basic) so a benign line
      like "Authorization failed for user 42" is left intact.
      NOTE: a literal '}' must NOT appear inside any %replace option even when quoted -
      Logback's OptionTokenizer tracks brace depth and an unbalanced '}' aborts pattern
      parsing (the \d{3,4} CVV mask is safe only because its braces are balanced). The
      ',' and '&quot;' inside the class are safe because single-quoting protects them.
      All appenders inherit this masking because they share ${CONSOLE_PATTERN}, which
      embeds ${MASKED_MSG}.
    -->
    <property name="MASKED_MSG"
        value="%replace(%replace(%replace(%replace(%replace(%replace(%replace(%msg){'(?i)(cvv[\\&quot;\s:=]*)\d{3,4}','$1***'}){'(?i)(ssn[\\&quot;\s:=]*)\d{3}-?\d{2}-?\d{4}','$1***-**-****'}){'\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b','***-**-****'}){'(?i)(password[\\&quot;\s:=]+)[^\s&quot;,&amp;]+','$1****'}){'(?i)(token[\\&quot;\s:=]+)[^\s&quot;,&amp;]+','$1****'}){'(?i)(bearer[\\&quot;\s:=]+)[^\s&quot;,&amp;]+','$1****'}){'(?i)(authorization[\\&quot;\s:=]+(?:bearer|basic)\s+)[^\s&quot;,&amp;]+','$1****'}"/>

    <!-- Shared layout pattern; ${MASKED_MSG} guarantees masking everywhere. -->
    <property name="CONSOLE_PATTERN"
        value="%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS} [%thread] %-5level %logger{36} - ${MASKED_MSG}%n"/>

    <!-- Console appender (masked). -->
    <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder>
            <pattern>${CONSOLE_PATTERN}</pattern>
            <charset>UTF-8</charset>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!--
      Per-profile log levels (Spring Boot extension).
      dev  : verbose application logging to the console.
      prod : quiet root (WARN) so framework/entity toString() output is not
             dumped; application logging at INFO; console + rolling file.
      else : conservative INFO console fallback for any other profile (e.g.
             "test", or when no profile is active).
    -->
    <springProfile name="dev">
        <logger name="com.carddemo" level="DEBUG"/>
        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

    <springProfile name="prod">
        <!--
          The rolling-file appender (masked) is defined inside the prod profile so it
          exists ONLY for the profile that references it. Defining it at the top level
          while referencing it only here would make Logback emit an "appender not
          referenced" warning under dev/test (which in turn triggers a full status
          dump on every startup). Size + time based rotation; same masked pattern as
          the console appender so the FILE output is masked too.
        -->
        <appender name="FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
            <file>${LOG_FILE}</file>
            <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
                <fileNamePattern>${LOG_PATH}/carddemo-%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log</fileNamePattern>
                <maxFileSize>10MB</maxFileSize>
                <maxHistory>30</maxHistory>
                <totalSizeCap>500MB</totalSizeCap>
            </rollingPolicy>
            <encoder>
                <pattern>${CONSOLE_PATTERN}</pattern>
                <charset>UTF-8</charset>
            </encoder>
        </appender>
        <logger name="com.carddemo" level="INFO"/>
        <root level="WARN">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
            <appender-ref ref="FILE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

    <springProfile name="!dev &amp; !prod">
        <logger name="com.carddemo" level="INFO"/>
        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

</configuration>
